Verdict from the evidence: the claim stands, not falsified. Key findings, all first-party code at commit 8f6fd5b on main:

- app/import/page.tsx:124-130: sold rows import as two independent network calls. The card is inserted and auto-committed first, then the sale insert is a separate call. No transaction anywhere: grep -rn rpc app/ lib/ is zero hits, and supabase-js has no transaction API.
- app/import/page.tsx:128-129: the sale insert's error is read exactly once, as a success counter (if (!s.error) sales++). Never passed to setErr, never logged. The loop continues to the next row.
- lib/importer.ts:279: the card draft is marked sold before any network call, so a failed sale insert leaves a committed sold card with zero sales rows, exactly the claim's failure state.
- app/import/page.tsx:132,166-168: the banner prints card count as all good rows and the sale count only of successes, so the loss is invisible unless someone compares two numbers with no prompt to do so.
- Schema rules out deterministic rejection: sales needs channel and price_cents, both supplied by construction (0001_init.sql:82-93); RLS on sales matches cards (0001_init.sql:136-137), so auth that passes the card insert passes the sale insert. Realistic failure modes are transient (network, 5xx, auth expiry), and none surface.
- Falsifier test: transaction wrap, no. Explicit failure surfacing, no. The card path surfaces its error via setErr three lines above, so the asymmetry looks like an oversight.
- Adjacent, same class, not the claim: the manual card editor can also mark status sold without a sale record (app/cards/[id]/page.tsx:191,209).

